    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d757">

                <pb facs="0938" n="930" sameAs="frus:frus1941v04/pg930"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">765.94/152: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rome</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-04-05T18:00:00+02:00">April 5, 1941—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 7:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>[469.] Embassy’s 456 and 457, April 4.<anchor n="42" xml:id="d757-f1"/> I have
                    learned on the best authority that during his conversations with the Pope and
                    the <pb facs="0939" n="931" xml:id="pg931"/> Cardinal Secretary of State
                    Matsuoka stressed the following points: (1) In China, Japan was conducting a
                    campaign against communism, not a war against the Chinese people, (2) an
                    extension and prolongation of the European war could only favor the cause of
                    communism, (3) Japan had no desire to be drawn into this war.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Berlin.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
